the percentage of female bus riders.\n\nWe need to find the grade where \\(M \\approx 1.35 \\times F\\).\n\n1. **9th Grade:**\n   - \\(M = 41.1\\%\\)\n   - \\(F = 39.4\\%\\)\n   - \\(1.35 \\times F = 1.35 \\times 39.4\\% = 53.29\\%\\)\n\n2. **10th Grade:**\n   - \\(M = 34.4\\%\\)\n   - \\(F = 33.1\\%\\)\n   - \\(1.35 \\times F = 1.35 \\times 33.1\\% = 44.635\\%\\)\n\n3. **11th Grade:**\n   - \\(M = 20.6\\%\\)\n   - \\(F = 13.8\\%\\)\n   - \\(1.35 \\times F = 1.35 \\times 13.8\\% = 18.57\\%\\)\n\n4. **12th Grade:**\n   - \\(M = 11.6\\%\\)\n   - \\(F = 8.6\\%\\)\n   - \\(1.35 \\times F = 1.35 \\times 8.6\\% = 11.61\\%\\)\n\nNow, let's calculate the absolute difference between the actual number of male riders and \\(1.35 \\times\\) the number of female riders for each grade:\n\n1. **9th Grade:**\n   - Difference = \\(|41.1\\% - 53.29\\%| = 12.19\\%\\)\n\n2. **10th Grade:**\n   - Difference = \\(|34.4\\% - 44.635\\%| = 10.235\\%\\)\n\n3. **11th Grade:**\n   - Difference = \\(|20.6\\% - 18.57\\%| = 2.03\\%\\)\n\n4. **12th Grade:**\n   - Difference = \\(|11.6\\% - 11.61\\%| = 0.01\\%\\)\n\nThe smallest difference is \\(0.01\\%\\), which occurs in the 12th grade.\n\nTherefore, the grade where the number of male bus riders is closest to \\(135\\%\\) of the number of female bus riders is \\(\\boxed{12\\text{th grade}}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{12\\text{th grade}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"prealgebra:544"}
